+### Durable human review persistence (API 29)
+
+Register `hunk.registerReviewPersistence({ open, write })` during factory registration to supply storage for native human notes.
+Exactly one extension may own persistence in a registry.
+`open(context)` receives the canonical `checkoutRoot`, logical `sourceLabel`, ordered stable file identities, and `documentIdentity`.
+Return `{ reviewId, checkpoint }` with an immutable review ID and an optional previously written checkpoint, or `null` to opt this input out of persistence.
+The host grants no storage directory or filesystem authority through this interface.
+An owner must bind its review IDs to the supplied checkout and document identity and acknowledge durable storage only when `write(checkpoint)` resolves.
+
+After validating and restoring a checkpoint, the host calls `write` with the complete rehydrated checkpoint at the new native generation and revision before exposing mounted lifecycle readiness.
+This also acknowledges the initial empty checkpoint when an owner opens a new review.
+A rejected acknowledgement preserves the existing durable checkpoint, shows a warning, and holds automatic following and human saves.
+Lifecycle `ctx.review` still exposes only `requestReload`; extensions obtain the acknowledged checkpoint through their registered persistence callback.
+
+Repeated flushes skip only a whole checkpoint already acknowledged by that controller, including its generation, revision, saved notes, composer, and selection.
+An unchanged outgoing viewer therefore cannot advance storage or overwrite a new viewer on close.
+A changed outgoing composer still attempts the owner's ordinary guarded write and must be refused when another viewer has advanced storage.
+Owners must retain independent read-time versions for each controller generation, including during preparation of a same-review reload; never refresh a stale version and blindly retry.
+
+Native Ctrl-S and mouse Save await that acknowledgement before publishing a saved human note.
+Failures preserve the composer text and display a persistent warning.
+Typing during a pending save is retained and requires another explicit save.
+Draft and selection checkpoints serialize with saved-note writes; graceful exit waits for started writes before extension shutdown.
+A sudden crash can restore the last acknowledged checkpoint, including its unsaved composer.
+
+`ctx.review.checkpoint()` returns a detached, deeply immutable checkpoint, or `null` without an active persistence identity or after the command generation expires.
+Version 1 checkpoints retain native human IDs, original anchors and source/context provenance, timestamps, open/resolved status, draft mode/body, semantic selection, and review identity.
+They exclude renderer geometry, source caches, live agent comments, and static sidecar notes.
+Restoration validates exact checkout, review ID, document identity, bounded fields, original context, note identities, and draft targets before restoring the shared store.
+Invalid or unreadable checkpoints remain untouched; later saves fail closed with a warning.
+Checkpoints are limited to 4 MiB and 2,048 saved human notes, with the ordinary note-size limit applying to each note.
+
+Saved-note snapshots include `filePath`, `fileAvailability: "available" | "unavailable"`, and native human `status: "open" | "resolved"` (default `open`).
+Missing addressed context becomes orphaned; changed source becomes stale.
+The host preserves original anchors and provenance without searching for nearby replacement lines.
+Notes whose files disappeared remain valid in public snapshots and live session registration, with unavailable-file and orphaned semantics and no navigable hunk.
+
+`await ctx.review.setNoteStatus(noteId, "resolved", expected)` changes a native human note's status through the shared controller and awaits durability when persistence is active.
+Saving a later edit reopens a resolved note while preserving its ID and original provenance.
+Supply `expected` from a current snapshot's `generation` and `stateRevision`.
+It returns `{ ok: true }` or `{ ok: false, reason, detail }`, where `reason` is `unavailable`, `conflict`, `busy`, or `failed`.
+The synchronous remote review action protocol cannot acknowledge storage, so human-save/update/delete/clear actions through that protocol are refused while persistence is registered.
+Agent comments and native editing continue through their existing shared paths.
+
+Command and event handlers may call `await ctx.review.requestReload(options)`.
+The default mode is `automatic`; it refuses an active draft, source editor, pending save, or unacknowledged open human note.
+`mode: "explicit"` permits leaving open saved notes after checkpointing, while still refusing active editing or saves.
+Both modes validate `expected: { generation, stateRevision }` before loading and recheck revision and editor activity at commit.
+Omitting `expected` captures the current position when the request is queued.
+A guarded refusal returns `busy`, `protected`, or `conflict`; storage/load failures return `failed` and expired controls return `unavailable`.
+Only option-free concurrent requests coalesce.
+
+`acknowledgedNotes: [noteId, ...]` releases protection for those exact captured human revisions without resolving their native status.
+This option requires an explicit `expected` generation/revision and known native human IDs.
+The caller must durably preserve or submit those revisions in its own workflow before acknowledging them.
+An edit changes the revision, so a previously captured acknowledgement cannot authorize replacement.
+Unacknowledged notes and all active composers/editors remain protected.
+This lets an extension retain submitted open notes while showing newer reviews, without confusing submission with resolution.
+
+To select another immutable review, supply `source: { kind: "patch", text }` with detached unified patch bytes (at most 16 MiB).
+Hunk keeps the logical source label and checkout identity, uses the ordinary patch parser, and restores only that incoming document's own checkpoint.
+An extension owns latest/cumulative/pinned history selection and feedback delivery; those workflows add no application protocol to Hunk.
+
+```ts
+hunk.registerCommand({ id: "resolve", title: "Resolve human note" }, async (ctx) => {
+  const snapshot = ctx.review.snapshot();
+  const note = snapshot?.notes.find((note) => note.source === "user");
+  if (!snapshot || !note) return;
+  const result = await ctx.review.setNoteStatus(note.id, "resolved", snapshot);
+  if (!result.ok) ctx.notify(result.detail, "warning");
+});
+```
