+
+```ts
+hunk.registerCommand({ id: "resolve", title: "Resolve human note" }, async (ctx) => {
+  const snapshot = ctx.review.snapshot();
+  const note = snapshot?.notes.find((note) => note.source === "user");
+  if (!snapshot || !note) return;
+  const result = await ctx.review.setNoteStatus(note.id, "resolved", snapshot);
+  if (!result.ok) ctx.notify(result.detail, "warning");
+});
+```
+
+## Implementation ownership
+
+`core/review/controller.ts` owns durable transactions over the existing ReviewStore and intent planner.
+`core/review/checkpoint.ts` validates checkpoints; `reconciliation.ts` preserves original provenance and classifies saved notes.
+`core/review/snapshot.ts` projects shared saved-note state, with the historical extension module re-exporting that projection.
+AppHost owns source loading, extension adoption, broker publication, and the existing React commit gate.
+The controller's commit ticket prevents awaited source loading from replacing a review that changed meanwhile.
+Public extension types remain import-free.
+The session daemon wire moves from 16 to 17 solely for explicit saved-note availability, resolution, and status inventory; local checkpoints are not a new wire protocol.
+
+## Verification boundary
+
+The isolated native PTY suite exercises keyboard and mouse save, restart/crash restoration, immutable review switching, guarded refusal, original-context rejection, stale/orphaned reconciliation, and public session registration.
+These tests use public extension controls and ordinary native Hunk sessions, with owner-supplied storage fault injection.
+The final command results and remaining visual-proof limitation are recorded in `turn-review-host-verification.md`.
+Pi, Codex, Herdr, packaging, activation, and feedback transport integration remain outside this host worktree's implementation scope.
